From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 15:51:27 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Wait on the right syscalls on riscv64 in
 _wait_until_process_blocks

`_wait_until_process_blocks` polls /proc/<pid>/syscall until the child is
parked in nanosleep/clock_nanosleep/connect/accept, but only knows the syscall
numbers for x86_64 and aarch64. Every other architecture falls back to a flat
one-second sleep, and on a slow machine that is not long enough: the client in
`test_live_client_exits_properly_on_sigint_before_connecting` has not reached
its `connect()` yet when SIGINT arrives, so the default handler kills it and
the test sees returncode -2 instead of 0.

riscv64 uses the asm-generic syscall table, which is the same one aarch64 uses,
so the numbers already in the aarch64 branch are correct for it: nanosleep 101,
clock_nanosleep 115, accept 202, connect 203 (scripts/syscall.tbl).

Upstream-Status: To upstream [not submitted from this automated port run; needs a pull request against bloomberg/memray]
---
 tests/integration/test_main.py | 3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/tests/integration/test_main.py b/tests/integration/test_main.py
index 7f72eec..dc2b248 100644
--- a/tests/integration/test_main.py
+++ b/tests/integration/test_main.py
@@ -94,7 +94,8 @@ def _wait_until_process_blocks(pid: int) -> None:
         connect_syscall = "42"
         accept_syscall = "43"
         clock_nanosleep = "230"
-    elif arch == "aarch64":
+    elif arch in ("aarch64", "riscv64"):
+        # Both use the asm-generic syscall table.
         sleep_syscall = "101"
         connect_syscall = "203"
         accept_syscall = "202"
